{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And Noah begot, etc.</b> It is difficult: first, why was it necessary to repeat a second time to say ״וַיּוֹלֶד״? After all, he wrote it in the previous portion (Genesis 5:32)! Second, why was it necessary to mention Noah's name rather than relying on its mention in the adjacent context? Third, why was it necessary to state the tally of their number, ״שְׁלֹשָׁה״? Fourth, there was no need to say ״בָּנִים״ — is it not understood from his saying ״וַיּוֹלֶד״ that the sons are his sons? Fifth, why did he write three instances of et?" "Now the intention is this: since the verse said ״אֵלֶּה תּוֹלְדֹת נֹחַ״ etc., to declare what we explained there — that the verse sets forth all the details of his good deeds through which Noah found grace in the eyes of HaShem — were the verse not to repeat and say ״וַיּוֹלֶד נֹחַ״ etc., there would be reason to say that it comes to disqualify the three sons, on the grounds that they possess no merit or virtue from themselves, and this would be for one of two reasons: either because they were wicked men deserving of death, or because Noah did not beget them for the sake of a commandment. Therefore the verse repeated and wrote them in the order of his good deeds, for this too he did as an aspect of his righteousness, to fulfill the commandment of his Creator. And I found in the Midrash (Yalkut Genesis 43) which says, and this is their language: Noah saw that they provoke the Holy One, blessed be He; he said, what business do I have exerting myself for procreation until age 500? He said, let the man die without sons — but HaShem said, ״פְּרוּ וּרְבוּ״, so he immediately engaged in procreation. See now — he begot them only for the sake of sustaining the species. And it appears to me that the verse should be examined according to the words of the Midrash in this manner: <b>et HaElohim walked Noah</b> — meaning, he saw that the time was near to walk in the way of all the earth; therefore he immediately married and begot three sons. Or in this manner: <b>et HaElohim</b> — in the sense of judgment (דַּיָּנוּת); he walked in the aspect of judgment and saw that he would be punished if he did not fulfill the commandment of procreation, and therefore ״וַיּוֹלֶד״ etc. It further intends, according to their words z״l (Genesis Rabbah 26:2), that he intended that the firstborn still not have reached the punishment of one hundred years. Accordingly, the verse intends to say <b>et HaElohim</b> — he judged by the judgment that his sons would be exempt according to the law, and then he begot them, so that they would not be punished when the Flood arrived, since they were not yet of punishable age." "And the reason it was necessary to mention his name time after time — according to our words it comes out correctly, for it intends to say that he produced repose (מְנוּחָה) of three sons through the wisdom he applied in begetting them in this order, so that they would not be of punishable age when the Flood came. And furthermore, truthfully, not only were they not of punishable age, yet even so they needed the merit of their father and through his intercession they were saved — and go out and learn from all the minors of the generation of the Flood who were not of punishable age and even so were destroyed together. And see what I wrote on the verse (Genesis 7:1) ״כִּי אוֹתְךָ רָאִיתִי צַדִּיק לְפָנַי״. And Noah their father produced for them a benefit against the enemies of their souls, the destroyers of the world, and not for them alone did he beget this progeny, but also for their three wives, who are alluded to in the three instances of et; and thus it says ״וּשְׁלֹשֶׁת נְשֵׁי בָנָיו״ — that they too were saved." "And the reason it was necessary to say ״שְׁלֹשָׁה בָנִים״ is explained according to what they said in Sanhedrin page 69a, and this is their language: how do you know that Scripture counts them according to their wisdom? For it is written (Genesis 5:32) ״וַיְהִי נֹחַ בֶּן ת״ק שָׁנָה וַיּוֹלֶד אֶת שֵׁם״ etc., and it is written (Genesis 11:10) ״אֵלֶּה תּוֹלְדוֹת שֵׁם, שֵׁם בֶּן מְאַת שָׁנָה וַיּוֹלֶד וגו׳ שְׁנָתַיִם אַחַר הַמַּבּוּל״ — he is a hundred and two years old! Rather, according to their wisdom Scripture counts them — end quote. And accordingly, the verse applied wisdom to say ״שְׁלֹשָׁה בָנִים״ to create a separation between the record of the begetting and the ordering of their names; for had it said ״וַיּוֹלֶד אֶת שֵׁם״ etc., it would have been heard as though it counts them in the order of their birth — but not so from his saying ״וַיּוֹלֶד שְׁלֹשָׁה בָנִים״: here it has finished declaring the begetting, and only afterward does the verse come to arrange them in the order worthy of being arranged, according to the order of their wisdom."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:54.950095525Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}